{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a ruler of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Levant from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was a member of the Anjou dynasty and a distant relative of the French kings.\n\n**Early Life and Reign Part 1**\nRaymond III was born around 1135 in Anjou France. He was the son of Fulk V Count of Anjou and Eremburga of Mainz. In 1152 Raymond III succeeded his cousin Raymond II Count of Tripoli who had died childless. Raymond III was crowned count shortly after his death. He ruled the county for nearly 35 years and expanded its borders through military campaigns.\n\n**Reign Part 2**\nDuring his reign Raymond III faced several challenges including conflicts with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Ayyubid dynasty. He also established trade relations with the Muslim world and encouraged the growth of trade and commerce in the county. In 1169 Raymond III was involved in the Siege of Abilene which was part of the Second Crusade. The siege failed to capture the city and Raymond III returned to Tripoli.\n\n**Later Life and Death Part 1**\nIn 1187 Raymond III died without an heir. He had previously designated his nephew Alfonso of Ibelin as his successor but Alfonso was unable to fulfill Raymond III's wishes due to his own death. The County of Tripoli was then occupied by the Ayyubid dynasty which was led by Saladin. Raymonds III legacy as a military leader and statesman was cemented by his efforts to protect the County of Tripoli from various threats during his long reign.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0016098171472549438}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001308832288486883}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.16895784437656403}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.802703940891661e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.0002967870968859643}, {"token": "oli", "logp